NOTE
Tutorial 3 — Issuing and Attesting a Credential.
Planning timestamps generated from the script's scene budgets.
Re-time against the final edit before publishing (see docs/video-tutorials.md).

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.400
Ledger data is public, so personal details
never go on-chain.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:14.400
The issuer keeps the credential document
off-chain and publishes only its

3
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.959
SHA-256 hash.

4
00:00:19.059 --> 00:00:25.959
Anyone holding the document can later
prove it matches the hash.

5
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:22.400
The university issues the credential.

6
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:29.400
It signs as the issuer, names the holder
as subject, sets credential type one

7
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:36.400
for a degree, and passes the metadata
hash.

8
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:43.400
The nonce prevents the same issuance from
being replayed.

9
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:50.400
The contract returns the credential ID.

10
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:07.400
Now the slice members attest.

11
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:14.400
Each attestor signs with its own key and
states true to confirm the credential.

12
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:21.400
We'll have the university and the society
attest.

13
00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:28.400
Their weights add up to eighty, which is
above our threshold of sixty.

14
00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:07.400
Check the result with is_attested; it
returns true.

15
00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:14.400
The REST endpoint shows the same, with the
attested weight and whether the

16
00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:21.400
answer came from cache.

17
00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:22.400
You issued a credential without putting
personal data on-chain, and collected

18
00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:28.761
enough attestations to reach quorum.

19
00:06:28.861 --> 00:06:35.761
Issuers should protect their keys with
hardware wallets; see the issuer security

20
00:06:35.861 --> 00:06:37.529
checklist.

21
00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:44.529
Next, we'll verify credentials as an
employer.
